67,3,1,talk,Misaki,,"Option A seems risky. George mentioned that monthly charges in other buildings led to arrears and social friction, which is exactly the kind of tension I want to avoid.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"Option B sounds like a lovely way to share, but I worry it might make the morning rush even more crowded. I'm afraid we might see more of those stressful shoving matches.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,1,talk,May,,"Option B is a dream for the treasurer! No billing, no chasing people for coins, and no bookkeeping nightmares. It's the only one that doesn't add to the administrative mess.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,1,talk,George,,"I agree with May. Option B is the only choice that doesn't create a permanent administrative burden. Option A is a recipe for conflict and arrears, and Option C requires a yearly reorganization that will eventually be neglected.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,1,talk,Mio,,"Option B sounds like a great way to stop the ""lucky"" spots from being held forever! But wait, if everyone just parks anywhere, will the bikes still be leaning on each other like Misaki mentioned?","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Mio, that is a valid concern. If we move to open racks without clear ownership, the leaning issues might continue, but this time without any way to identify who is responsible, potentially increasing the frustration.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"I agree with Misaki. If we don't have a gentle way to manage the spaces, I worry that the bikes will just end up pushed together, and some of our neighbors' lovely bicycles might get scratched.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,2,talk,May,,"@Shizue, a few scratches are a small price to pay for not having a financial meltdown! Option A is out of the question—we can't afford the social or administrative cost of chasing payments.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,2,talk,George,,"@Shizue, while scratches are unfortunate, they are a physical problem we can solve with better racks. The administrative collapse of a payment system or a neglected lottery is a systemic problem that is much harder to fix.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@George, you're right about the system! But if we go with Option B, we have to make sure the racks are actually good. I just want everyone to feel like they have a fair shot at a dry spot!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"Option C is also problematic. A lottery might seem fair, but it creates a yearly cycle of winners and losers, which could lead to resentment and awkwardness in the lobby when the results are announced.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"I understand, Misaki. A lottery can feel like a game of chance, and it would be so sad if some neighbors felt unlucky for a whole year. But I still worry that Option B might lead to more shoving in the mornings.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,3,talk,May,,"@Shizue, let's be honest, a little morning energy is better than a bookkeeping disaster! Option B is the only one that doesn't turn our treasurer into a debt collector. It's clean, simple, and totally free!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,3,talk,George,,"@May, exactly. Option B removes the human element of policing payments or managing a list. It is the only sustainable path that doesn't rely on one person's endless patience or a rigid schedule that will eventually be ignored.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
81,3,3,talk,Mio,,"@Misaki, if Option C just makes people feel unlucky, then Option B is way better! It's like a fresh start every day! As long as we get those double-tier racks George mentioned, it should be fair and exciting!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Misaki"",""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
82,3,4,talk,Misaki,,"I agree that Option B is the most stable choice. While the leaning issues might persist, it avoids the financial friction of A and the yearly resentment of C. I'll vote for Option B.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
83,3,4,talk,Shizue,,"I still have a little worry about the morning rush, but if we all agree to be gentle with one another, Option B could be a wonderful way to share. I'll vote for Option B.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
84,3,4,talk,May,,"I'm absolutely voting for B! It's the only option that doesn't turn our building into a bank or a casino. Let's keep it simple, free, and stress-free for the treasurer!","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
85,3,4,talk,George,,I have no reason to disagree. Option B is the only one that ensures the system won't collapse the moment someone gets tired of the paperwork. I'll vote for Option B.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
86,3,4,talk,Mio,,"I'm totally on board! Option B is the perfect way to shake things up and make sure nobody is stuck in the rain forever. It's fair, simple, and exciting! I vote for Option B!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
